/roller.plugin.ts
Purpose: Automatically accepts or rejects stat rolls based on configurable thresholds and prompts.

/speech-text.ts
Purpose: Sanitizes text bound for a speech engine — strips the full escape-sequence set (any CSI final byte, private-mode toggles, OSC strings, a sequence cut off by a chunk boundary, stray control bytes), not just the SGR colors the other stripAnsi helpers remove, so the synthesizer never reads codes aloud.

/standup.plugin.ts
Purpose: Enables automatic standing up in game when specific knockdown phrases are detected.

/__fixtures__/room-render-corpus.json
Purpose: Verbatim room_data windows extracted from the docker game-server jsonl corpus (C:\Projects\DSL\GameLogs\ShatteredArchive\Docker\game-server) — each case is the text emitted since the previous prompt, the room name the trailing room_data carried, the command that produced it, and renderStart (the index where the render begins, -1 = none). Deliberately real rather than hand-written: both bugs it guards against came from believing a plausible story about the protocol instead of measuring it. THREE shapes on purpose — plain renders (renderStart 0), PREFIXED renders (renderStart > 0: portal echoes, "You step through a gate and vanish.", umbral corridors) which are the 16%-miss regression and the reason the fixture was regenerated, and non-render windows (equipment lists, chat, improve messages, "You are thirsty.") that must NOT be filed as exploration. The first fixture held only the plain and non-render shapes, which is exactly why the miss passed its tests.

/text-to-speech.plugin.test.ts
Purpose: Pins room-render detection against that real corpus fixture (every render recognised, every non-render window rejected, first-line anchoring so a chat line merely quoting a room name cannot confirm one, and empty-held/empty-name refusals since room_data fires before prompts with no preceding text), the command gate's anchoring (map never gating unmap/maple/"say the map is wrong", whitespace collapse, uncompilable-entry fallback), the rate-aware utterance math — that maxUtteranceCharsFor keeps an unassisted utterance under Chrome's ~15s stall at every rate, scales with rate, reaches ~1000 chars with the keepalive on, floors at 80 so a slow rate still yields a usable chunk, and survives a junk rate (a NaN cap makes `length > cap` false and sends an unsplit block straight to the engine) — plus splitForSpeech's sentence/whitespace/mid-word break preference.

/text-to-speech.plugin.ts
Purpose: Reads text aloud via browser speechSynthesis — auto-reads the play area (filtered, capped, respects line-squelch, auto-skips PROMPT/status lines via a vitals-shape heuristic since format is per-player, groups bursts within a debounced window into one utterance joined by spaces so word-wrap doesn't add pauses) and/or an on-demand floating button that reads selected/focused page text; also optional spoken alerts on self/enemy health-tier changes (GMCP-driven, deduped), on game:tick (with the in-game time from that same event), and on self-stun (reuses Stun Highlight's text matchers — CharData.stance in types-global is NOT a position/incapacitation flag, it's the offensive/normal/defensive combat-stance toggle, so it's not a usable stun signal; when Combat Mode is Battle Focus this same toggle also drives self-debuff callouts from game:affect-added/removed, using the modifier's sign — inverted for ac — to tell debuff from buff, skipping zero-modifier binary effects like stuns/charms). Combat Mode ('off' | 'battleFocus'): Battle Focus is a "game caster" mode for the player's own fights sharing speakArenaLineIfWorthy's classify-and-speak pipeline with Arena Observer Mode below (squelches Damage/Avoidance/Ambient/Item Use/Attack Flavor/UI Noise/weapon-flag procs, speaks Status/Incapacitation/Disarm/Death/Flee immediately, dedupes repeats by canonicalized shape) but with condition-tier lines dropped rather than spoken (that's the self/enemy health-alert toggles' job, avoiding a double announcement) and chat lines left alone (this is the player's own conversation, not spectator noise). Also Arena Observer Mode, a "game caster" mode covering BOTH DSL arena types via one self-contained pipeline (bypasses the normal squelch/prompt/Combat-Mode/include-exclude chain entirely): Coliseum is spectator-only, gated per-line on the five Coliseum rooms' broadcast prefix (no "you" tracking); Bloodbath is participant-driven, gated on the player's current room (tracked off the vitals-prompt line, which also closes a room-look-render squelch block opened by the Exits line), with "Bloodbath System:" announcements always speaking regardless of room and the repeating "You sure are BLEEDING!" nag deduped to one mention. Both arena paths ALSO drop chat/speech lines entirely (via strict-chat-classifier.ts's classifyStrictChatSubtype — say/tell/yell/gossip/OOC/etc.), unlike Battle Focus mode which leaves them alone — built from Coliseum (room names via C:\Projects\DSL\AGL\Text\2024-02-17_Piknim_Pardo.txt) and Bloodbath (room mechanics via C:\Projects\DSL\GameLogs\GameLog-DSL_2023-11-10-Fri.txt) log corpus mining passes. Speech pipeline: include/exclude patterns are regex-capable (literal-substring fallback on invalid syntax); utterance-start delay (default 150ms) + a resume()/cancel() nudge plus a near-silent primer utterance queued immediately ahead of each real one, absorbing Chrome/Edge's start-of-buffer clip (reported to still cut into the first word even without a prior utterance or idle period) instead of the real utterance eating it. Controls are ONE draggable floating group (#text-to-speech-controls, column-reverse so mute keeps the original bottom-left anchor and inherits its old saved position): mute (stop + clear queue), pause/play (a HELD engine pause that DISCARDS rather than buffers — discardQueued() drops the queue and the half-built group at pause, enqueue() drops everything arriving while paused incl. the read-selection button, so play resumes live commentary not a backlog; only the in-flight utterance survives and continues. pump, the keepalive tick, and the pre-speak resume() nudge all check userPaused, and the stall watchdog re-arms instead of firing so a long hold never "recovers" past the paused line), and a combat-mode toggle. makeDraggable now defers setPointerCapture until the drag passes the slop threshold — capturing on pointerdown retargets the terminating click away from the individual bubble. The five switches collapse behind ONE 🎛️ bubble that pops out a labelled panel (absolutely positioned so opening never resizes the drag target, anchored to the button so it follows the group, and flipped leftward when the group sits near the right edge); the collapsed button's tooltip summarises what is currently spoken. Speech is FOUR INDEPENDENT category switches (speakExploration/speakBattle/speakCommunication/speakIncidental), not exclusive modes — they compose, so "battle + chat, rooms silent" is normal. 'incidental' is the catch-all (ambient room echoes, improvement lines, random stat flavor, anything unmatched); switching it OFF is what makes the reader an allowlist. Arena (arenaObserverMode) stays EXCLUSIVE and overrides all four, since its room-gated pipeline bypasses the chain those switches live in. resolveSpeechToggles reads absent keys as ON (a pre-switch config would otherwise go mute); a one-time onEnable migration expands a stored speechMode/combatMode via togglesForLegacyMode and nulls both keys — neither is in defaults, which is the ONLY way a plugin can tell a stored value from a default. Affliction callouts are debounced per target+effect on a 6s CLOCK (AFFLICTION_REPEAT_MS), not on "was this the last thing said": a stun re-echoes every round while the target is down, with ordinary combat lines in between, so the old adjacency check let every round through. Superseded design, kept for history — the earlier exclusive-mode scheme and Arena, which absorbed the old standalone arenaObserverMode checkbox (resolveMode maps a saved `arenaObserverMode: true` onto it, outranking a stored combatMode exactly as the old early-return did; an explicit speechMode still wins). Arena keeps its early return — its room-gated pipeline bypasses the squelch/prompt/category/include-exclude chain entirely and is NOT additive with the other modes. Modes (key 'speechMode', falling back to the stored 'combatMode'; legacy 'off' and anything unknown normalize to 'all', deliberately NOT 'exploration', so nobody silently loses chat/combat): every play-area line is sorted by classifySpeechCategory into exploration/battle/communication/other and MODE_CATEGORIES decides which the mode speaks. 'other' is in EVERY mode — a mode narrows what is filtered out, it is not a whitelist. Battle lines always route through speakArenaLineIfWorthy incl. in 'all' (Battle Focus IS the battle feature; "all on" does not mean raw damage spam). Exploration detection is CONFIRMED BY A TRAILING MARKER AND DISCRIMINATED BY THE ROOM NAME, corrected 2026-08-02 against the docker game-server jsonl corpus (207 files, 341,481 room_data events). A room render (title, description, exits, optional creatures/objects) IS always immediately followed by game:room-data, but the converse is FALSE and assuming it cost a rewrite: DSL emits room_data + char_data before EVERY prompt, and only 9.1% of those events follow a render — the other 78.9% trail equipment lists, auction chat, skill-improve messages, "You are thirsty.", with 12% following no text at all. So the event's ARRIVAL confirms nothing; the NAME discriminates. roomRenderStartIndex SCANS the held batch for the last line that IS the room name and returns its index (-1 = no render) — scanning, not anchoring on held[0], because a second corpus pass (2026-08-02, prompted by the fix still sounding wrong live) showed the title is frequently NOT the first line: a first-line anchor missed 6,170 of 37,997 real renders, 16.2%, one room in six. Portal/enter echoes ("You enter a dark glowing orb.", 23% of portal renders), "You wake and stand up.", escape flavor ("Just as you start to leave, a fountain of blood erupts from the altar."), and interleaved chat all land ahead of the title; 15.7% of all real renders are prefixed this way. Scanning drops the miss rate to 0.74% (283, mostly scrying "You peer into a shimmering gate." and ship-movement renders, where room_data names YOUR room rather than the one being described). LAST match, not first, since a window can hold several renders (river drift, speedwalk, login dump) and the event describes the final one. Whole-line equality only, never containment, so a chat line quoting a room name cannot open one — corpus-clean, every matched window also carried an `[Exits:]` line bar 20 short brief-mode renders that legitimately have none. Lines BEFORE the index are released as 'incidental', not 'exploration': "You enter a dark glowing orb." is how you learn the portal worked, and silencing room descriptions must not silence it. Because the marker TRAILS, no line can be classified on sight — so a line classified 'incidental' (the catch-all, where an unrecognised title/description lands) is HELD in roomCandidateLines for ROOM_RENDER_CONFIRM_MS (250ms; MEASURED, not guessed — render-start→room_data is p50 1ms, p95 5ms, p99 8ms, max 89ms, nothing above 250) instead of being decided: a name-matching room-data releases the batch SPLIT at the render index (prefix as 'incidental', render onward as 'exploration'), anything else (or the timer elapsing) releases the whole batch as 'incidental'. Only incidental lines wait; battle/communication/already-open-exploration go straight through. The old bug this fixes: the title arrives BEFORE the room-data naming it, so on a room's FIRST visit the recentRoomNames ring is empty and nothing identifies the block — the Exits line opened it, but the title and whole description had already been classified and spoken, so every new room read itself out regardless of the exploration switch. The ring + Exits opener both remain, no longer load-bearing but keeping repeat visits classifiable at arrival with zero delay; room-data also now clears inRoomBlock (the render is over; the prompt that used to be its only terminator may still be several lines away). Resolution still honours the resolved category's toggle, so this is a classification fix and not a bypass. Held lines are a THIRD buffer behind the queue and the group, and discardQueued/disable clear all three — pause must not surface a room description a quarter-second later. Tracked ABOVE the include/exclude filters since it is structure, not speech policy. Communication = classifyStrictChatSubtype's 20 channels, tested FIRST so chat interleaved into a render stays chat. The mode bubble cycles all four and persists via pluginConfigPersist since updateConfig alone is reverted by syncInstalled. classifyEnemyAffliction (19 matchers, expanded 2026-08-02) turns third-person condition echoes into short callouts ("Grumbly is stunned.") and runs BEFORE isArenaSquelchLine. ADMISSION RULE: a line is admitted only if knowing it CHANGES WHAT YOU DO — lost rounds (bash/paralysis/stun/toss), degraded offence (blind incl. the spit variant, slow, disarm, shield stripped), degraded defence or sustain (faerie fire, poison landing, plague landing, drained healing) — plus, new, BUFFS on your opponent under their own `announceEnemyBuffs` toggle (sanctuary halves your damage, stone skin, haste, antimagic shell, charm breaking), which were previously dropped wholesale as "not afflictions". Two things are corpus-verified and deliberately EXCLUDED: per-round ticks ("X shivers and suffers." 6,385 hits, plague sores 844) because the landing is the news and the tick is that news on repeat, and warcry (241 hits) because its mechanical effect cannot be stated. Every matcher is parsed-from-source verified against real traffic — all 19 fire, 0 dead. That pass caught two mis-captures a hand-written test would not: the lifebane "coats" line is "<player> coats <weapon>", NOT "<weapon> coats <victim>" as both the reference and PatternMap had it (nobody is afflicted; dropped), and the plague landing needed anchoring because the combined ". shivers and looks very ill." form made an unanchored pattern announce "shivers and" as a combatant — the Stunning weapon-flag knockdown and both poison procs are weapon-flag lines that Battle Focus was dropping wholesale as proc noise. Its patterns come from the already-corpus-verified Combat Compression + Weapon Flag tables, NOT from the community reference's ~90-spell affect table, which is written victim-side ("You are blinded!") and cannot detect anything landing on someone else. It reports WHO from the line text and has no notion of your target, so a groupmate reads the same as an enemy. Utterance sizing is RATE-AWARE, not a fixed character cap: Chrome/Edge stall at ~15s of continuous speech (a TIME limit — the spec has no length limit), so maxUtteranceCharsFor(rate, keepalive) derives the cap from ~18 chars/sec × rate — ~200 chars at rate 1 unassisted, ~990 with the keepalive on — and the stall watchdog's budget comes off the same estimate (the old flat 5s+120ms/char fired mid-utterance at slow rates and spoke the next line over the top). The 'Long-utterance keepalive' toggle (default on) arms a 10s pause()/resume() tick, WALL-CLOCK since the engine's cutoff is, armed only when the rate-aware duration estimate exceeds the unassisted window so short-at-high-rate utterances skip its audible hitch. Every pump/watchdog/utterance/init diagnostic is behind the `debug` config toggle (the `interrupted` utterance error in particular fires on every normal stop/cancel); only direct responses to a user action — the read-selection failure, the voices listing — print unconditionally. Command gate (commandGateEnabled/commandGatePatterns/commandGateMs, default on, `map` + `map \d+`, 500ms): an INPUT-keyed silencer for commands whose output is layout rather than words (map grids, tables, ASCII art) — the inverse of an exclude pattern, which matches output you can describe, and the only workable approach when the noise has no stable text. Observed via onAlias, which never consumes the command (returns undefined) and therefore only sees input NO user-script alias claimed first — a user who aliased `map` gates nothing, documented in the field description rather than worked around. matchesCommandGate is IMPLICITLY ANCHORED (`^(?:entry)$`, case-insensitive, input whitespace-collapsed so the plain `map \d+` still fires on `map   3`): a substring match would gate unmap/maple/"say the map is wrong", blanking speech with no visible cause — `map.*` is available for a deliberate prefix match, an uncompilable entry falls back to exact equality, and #-comments/blanks are skipped. Held as a wall-clock DEADLINE (commandGateUntil), not a boolean+timer, so nothing needs cancelling on disable/pause/re-arm and a second gated command pushes the deadline out via max() rather than shortening it; reset to 0 on disable so a live gate can't outlast it. Checked in BOTH queueLine and enqueue and the duplication is load-bearing — grouping can carry a line that ARRIVED inside the window past a flush that lands outside it (400ms of map body + a 300ms window flushes at ~700ms, past a 500ms gate), and dropping on arrival also stops gated rows dragging a following legitimate line into the same discarded utterance. Arming also stopSpeaking()+discardQueued(), since anything mid-flight predates the command.

/voice-dictation.plugin.ts
Purpose: Speech-to-text dictation (merged 2026-07-21 from the former voice-dictation-engine + voice-dictation-input pair into one config flow) — chooses the recognition engine (browser Web Speech API vs. downloadable wav2vec2, literal/non-generative), language, model, and custom vocabulary, then dictates into either the command input (reclaims the hidden mic slot in CommandInput.tsx via an inline display-style override, not injected CSS) or any other focused page text field. usePlugins.ts migrates existing installs of the old two plugin ids into this one on load.

/warlock-alphabet.plugin.ts
Purpose: This plugin helps warlocks manage spell recipes by tracking item assignments to brew letters and automating correct insertion order.

/weapon-flag-squelch.plugin.ts
Purpose: Squelches weapon-flag proc echo lines (Frost/Flaming/Shocking/Vampiric/Stunning/Mana Drain/Holy/Unholy/Poison) via patterns verified against the real server log corpus; exports PATTERNS for reuse (e.g. text-to-speech's combat compression).
